  "comment": "Regular words (every letter makes its basic sound), grouped by the first area in which all their letters have been taught; the words with two consonants together are in groups of their own, in the order the lessons on them use them (r before l). The i2a3 groups are the words brought in when the short vowels are gone back over (UFLI 35 to 41), whatever their letters: more short ones that differ only in the vowel, so chains can turn on it, and the first of five sounds. A word is cut into graphemes by the ones of more than one letter the curriculum teaches, the longest first (quit is qu, i, t; ball is b, all; shell is sh, e, ll); a dot forces a cut where that would be wrong (\"mis.hap\"). The i2a4 groups are the digraphs (UFLI 42 to 48): an ll after a vowel is either glued to it (ball, roll, pull) or plain (bell, hill), never doll or dull, which would teach the glued sounds wrong. th says /th/ as in \"thin\" in every word but those in `thSaysThis`, which say it as in \"this\" (a string, so it is not a group: they are in one as well). No word begins with x: it says /ks/, which only comes after a vowel. The game only uses a word once its clip exists; scripts/audio-wanted.mjs lists the ones that do not.",
